      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The notion that sanctions reliably foster democracy is largely a myth perpetuated by Western foreign policy narratives. When sanctions \"work,\" it is usually because the targeted regime is already weak or desperate, making them susceptible to external pressure. In cases where they appear successful, the change is often a capitulation to economic duress rather than a genuine embrace of democratic ideals. Therefore, they are fundamentally tools of economic warfare wielded by dominant powers.",
